#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172908} :activity/id #uuid "1cf8d435-d537-5b08-8592-19448de0ebaa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048656 :topic/id #uuid "b2c975cf-4aab-5305-b223-4eaed44fd857" :topic/math-academy-id 3338} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173162 :tutorial/content "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a0eebf3c-594c-5d34-868e-6c476f587498" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5092 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fd3fa277-366a-51f9-a9dd-222993535dab" :step/math-academy-id 31935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172044 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171850 :question/id #uuid "dd9a6e6a-e3d1-5684-8a14-7da410461aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414227}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "392b768a-c4a0-5b11-884b-71eb53759374" :step/math-academy-id 32081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172045 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171851 :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414224}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "c1002b1a-1dd1-5a5c-addc-3f3958efbc84" :step/math-academy-id 32082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172046 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171852 :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050614}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414225}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "3cf60778-9584-5fa1-a983-0b811161a21e" :step/math-academy-id 32083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173205 :tutorial/content "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\omega t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." :tutorial/id #uuid "04a3ea53-9923-588e-b205-6980447b9231" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5805 :tutorial/title "Linear vs. Angular Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "b328c2b2-b79d-5bf1-9c7d-2b99b0ab3382" :step/math-academy-id 35574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173260 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d24aa67f-988c-52f1-89ef-bb9571593fa1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7810 :tutorial/title "The Acceleration Vector"} :step/id #uuid "f5f2037c-c390-5f31-b9b4-856ee301062a" :step/math-academy-id 45177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172915}}] :activity/title "Circular Motion About the Origin" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172168}]}
